    "body": "Photo: VesselFinder\nBy Alison Koo\n17 November 2026\nCharter prices for containerships continue to defy the cooldown in long-haul freight rates, as major operators continue to hunt tonnage.\nLinerlytica this week reported that French line CMA CGM took the 2001-built 5,610 teu Aigio (previously known as Wan Hai 611 ) for 3.5 years.\nWhile the rate was not disclosed, charter rates for similar-size vessels are around $66,000 a day, up 10% year on year.\nAigio is one of three sister ships Wan Hai sold in March for $34m to $38m each, Greek tonnage provider Costamare buying two and CMA CGM the other – Wan Hai 612 and Wan Hai 613 have not been renamed or delivered yet.\nCharter rates for box ships of all sizes have risen by between 7% and 23% year on year, with feeder vessels registering the biggest gains due to higher demand from intra-region trades and the lack of newbuilds in recent years. A 1,700 teu ship is now going for around $32,000 a day.\n“The strong demand in the containership charter market continues, with charter rates still edging upwards, with forward fixtures stretching into the end of 2027,” Linerlytica said.\nCMA CGM has also been busy in the second-hand market, having bought the 2017-built 2,506 teu Nordamelia from Reederei Nord at an undisclosed price. Since 2020, the carrier has bought 178 ships, narrowing the capacity gap with Maersk to just 130,000 teu.\nMeanwhile MSC, by far the busiest buyer of second-hand ships, continues to hunt for tonnage to consolidate its lead over its peers.\nThe Geneva-headquartered operator lately bought the 2011-built 5,550 teu MSC Cadiz , after chartering it from Claus-Peter Offen for 15 years, taking its fleet to 7.46m teu.\nSince the pandemic sent freight and charter rates to historical highs, MSC has acquired 521 ships, for 1.6m teu.",
